## What the license does not cover

Wordwinnow depends on packages, data, and services it does not own; for each,
this says what the dependency is, whose content it is, which terms apply, and
what credit Wordwinnow gives.

**Dependencies.** Every third-party package the project installs, and every
container image the Compose stack pulls, keeps its own license, and none of
them is changed by this notice.

**Reference word lists.** The CEFR-J Vocabulary Profile and the Octanove
Vocabulary Profile in `data/reference/` ship in this repository unchanged,
keep their authors' terms, and are credited as those terms ask, all stated in
`data/reference/ATTRIBUTION.md`; the level model learns its labels from them
under those terms, and from no other labeled data.

**Sample story.** Arthur Conan Doyle's "A Scandal in Bohemia" in
`data/corpus/` is in the public domain, and the Standard Ebooks edition it was
taken from dedicates its editors' work to the public domain under CC0 1.0, as
`data/corpus/README.md` records with its source.

**WordNet.** Princeton University's WordNet 3.0 is read at run time from the
copy `make nltk-data` downloads from a fixed commit of the NLTK project's data
repository, and it does not ship in this repository; the senses, glosses, and
relations a report shows are WordNet's, under the WordNet 3.0 license, which a
report names when WordNet serves as its dictionary.

The tests quote a few WordNet glosses word for word, to check the adapters
that read them; the WordNet license permits copying on condition that its
notice accompanies the copies, and that notice closes this file.

**Word frequencies.** The frequencies come from the `wordfreq` package, which
the project installs rather than ships, and whose data is licensed CC BY-SA
4.0.

**The Free Dictionary API.** When the dictionary is chosen, Wordwinnow calls
the Free Dictionary API, <https://dictionaryapi.dev>, a free service built and
run by its developer, `meetDeveloper`
(<https://github.com/meetDeveloper/freeDictionaryAPI>).

Three different things apply to it, and only the second is an obligation:

- The service itself: it publishes no terms of use and asks for no
  attribution, and the GPL-3.0 license of its repository covers the service's
  own code, which Wordwinnow neither contains nor distributes, so nothing
  obliges Wordwinnow to credit the service.
- The content it returns: the definitions and examples are Wiktionary's, under
  the license the API reports with each entry, CC BY-SA 3.0, which requires
  attribution wherever the content is reproduced, so a report names that
  license under the words and gives the Wiktionary page of each definition
  shown.
- Courtesy: this credit to the service and its developer, and the one in
  `docs/product.md`, is given because Wordwinnow relies on a free service, not
  because any terms require it.

An analysis stores what the API returned alongside its result, with that
license and those pages, and none of it ships in this repository; the Free
Dictionary definitions in the tests were written for the tests rather than
copied.

**New York Times content.** Headlines, abstracts, lead paragraphs, links, and
feed items the `nyt-*` sources fetch are third-party content under The New
York Times API Terms of Service and the NYTimes.com Terms of Service;
Wordwinnow does not relicense them, shows them with the attribution the terms
require, lets nobody read an analysis of them after one day and deletes it
before any later read, and never trains, fits, or evaluates anything on them.

**The engineering conventions standard.** Wordwinnow follows the engineering
conventions standard, a separate project by the same copyright holder, and
does not depend on it at run time; `tools/check_conventions.py`,
`tools/check_commit_message.py`, and `.taplo.toml` are unchanged copies of its
enforcement files, and the Ruff tables `pyproject.toml` marks as copied come
from it, all under that project's Apache License 2.0, which the two checkers
name in their own headers and which does not extend to the rest of Wordwinnow.

